{{/* @dot github.com/donseba/go-partial/examples/docs.DocsPage */}}
<article class="grid max-w-5xl gap-6">
    <p class="leading-7 text-slate-400 text-[13px] font-extrabold uppercase text-emerald-300">Guide</p>
    <h1 class="max-w-4xl text-5xl font-extrabold leading-tight text-slate-50">Error boundaries</h1>
    <p class="leading-7 text-slate-400 max-w-4xl text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300">A failing registered partial renders a section-level fallback. The page shell survives, which is useful for development and production.</p>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-50">Default behavior</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-slate-400">Direct top-level failures render a useful error page. Region failures render an error section inside the parent output.</p>
    <p class="leading-7 text-slate-400">The optional <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">ext/errors</code> stage focuses on the template failure itself. In detailed mode it shows the partial ID, template list, request URL, extracted template location, and original render error.</p>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-50">Where boundaries apply</h2>
    <table class="w-full overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-slate-700 bg-slate-800/90 text-left">
        <thead class="bg-slate-900"><tr><th class="border-b border-slate-700 px-3 py-2.5 text-left font-bold text-slate-50">Failure</th><th class="border-b border-slate-700 px-3 py-2.5 text-left font-bold text-slate-50">Result</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td class="border-b border-slate-700 px-3 py-2.5 align-middle">Top-level content fails during <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">partial.Write</code></td><td class="border-b border-slate-700 px-3 py-2.5 align-middle">A full error page for normal requests, or a swappable error fragment for HTMX requests.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="border-b border-slate-700 px-3 py-2.5 align-middle">Registered region fails inside a parent template</td><td class="border-b border-slate-700 px-3 py-2.5 align-middle">The parent keeps rendering and the region position receives an error fragment.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="border-b border-slate-700 px-3 py-2.5 align-middle">Selected partial fails inside <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">{{ "{{ selection }}" }}</code></td><td class="border-b border-slate-700 px-3 py-2.5 align-middle">The selected tab or panel receives an error fragment while the rest of the page survives.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="border-b border-slate-700 px-3 py-2.5 align-middle"><code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">RenderWithRequest</code> returns an error</td><td class="border-b border-slate-700 px-3 py-2.5 align-middle">The caller decides how to handle it. The embedded page is a writer-level behavior.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-50">Development and production</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-slate-400">The default extension mode is <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">errors.ModeSafe</code>. It shows the status text and a safe message, and names no partial, template or error. During development, opt into <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">errors.ModeDetailed</code> to show the partial ID, template list, URL, template location, and original error.</p>

    <pre class="overflow-auto rounded-lg border border-slate-700 bg-slate-950 p-4 text-slate-100 shadow-2xl"><code class="block border-0 bg-transparent p-0 font-mono text-sm text-inherit">root := partial.New("shell.gohtml").
    Use(errors.Stage(errors.WithMode(errors.ModeDetailed)))</code></pre>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-50">Custom stage</h2>
    <pre class="overflow-auto rounded-lg border border-slate-700 bg-slate-950 p-4 text-slate-100 shadow-2xl"><code class="block border-0 bg-transparent p-0 font-mono text-sm text-inherit">root := partial.New("shell.gohtml").
    Use(partial.RenderStageHooks{
        RenderFunc: func(ctx *partial.RenderContext, next partial.RenderNext) (template.HTML, error) {
            if ctx.Kind != errors.RenderKindError {
                return next(ctx)
            }
            return template.HTML("&lt;section&gt;Something went wrong&lt;/section&gt;"), nil
        },
    })</code></pre>

    <p class="leading-7 text-slate-400">The separate showcase app includes a live broken-template example. It keeps the shell in place and only replaces the failing content section.</p>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-50">Failing the whole response</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-slate-400">By default a failing part of a page renders as its error fragment in place. With <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">FailResponse</code>, any failing or missing part fails the render: <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">Render</code>, <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">RenderWithRequest</code> and <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">RenderSelf</code> return the first failure and no HTML, and <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">Write</code> writes only the failure response of the error stage, or nothing without one, and returns the error.</p>
    <pre class="overflow-auto rounded-lg border border-slate-700 bg-slate-950 p-4 text-slate-100 shadow-2xl"><code class="block border-0 bg-transparent p-0 font-mono text-sm text-inherit">root.SetFailurePolicy(partial.FailResponse)

err := partial.Write(r.Context(), w, r, page)
var written *partial.FailureResponseError
if err != nil &amp;&amp; !errors.As(err, &amp;written) {
    // Nothing was written; answer with an error page.
}</code></pre>
    <p class="leading-7 text-slate-400">When <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">Write</code> wrote the failure response of an error stage, it returns a <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">*partial.FailureResponseError</code> with the status it wrote. Failure responses are sent with <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">Cache-Control: no-store</code>. With <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">FailResponse</code> the failure response keeps an error status for partial requests too and comes without out-of-band regions; with <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">FailInline</code> a partial request gets the error fragment with status 200, so the client swaps it in place.</p>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-50">Error templates of your own</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-slate-400">The failure response can be a template of the application, such as a theme's 500 page, and the error fragment a toast. The template renders as a child of the partial that failed, with its file system, functions, includes and models; its dot is an <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">exterrors.Data</code>. Show <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">.Message</code> only when <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">.Detailed</code>. When the template fails itself, the built-in markup answers.</p>
    <pre class="overflow-auto rounded-lg border border-slate-700 bg-slate-950 p-4 text-slate-100 shadow-2xl"><code class="block border-0 bg-transparent p-0 font-mono text-sm text-inherit">root.Use(exterrors.Stage(
    exterrors.WithTemplate("500.gohtml"),
    exterrors.WithFragmentTemplate("partials/error-toast.gohtml"),
))</code></pre>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-50">Errors as flash messages</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-slate-400">With <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">exterrors.WithFlash(fn)</code>, a partial request that fails is answered with an error flash message, out of band in the flash target, with the template of the tree's <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">exp/flash</code> stage, and the target stays as it is. <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">fn</code> gives the text, such as a translated one; a nil <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">fn</code> shows <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">Data.Message</code>. Under <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">FailResponse</code> the status stays 500: with htmx 4, let the page swap the out-of-band parts of 5xx responses with <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">hx-status:5xx="swap:none"</code> (<code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">connector.Attr.KeepOnServerError</code>).</p>
    <pre class="overflow-auto rounded-lg border border-slate-700 bg-slate-950 p-4 text-slate-100 shadow-2xl"><code class="block border-0 bg-transparent p-0 font-mono text-sm text-inherit">root.Use(
    flash.Stage(flash.WithTemplate("templates/flash.gohtml")),
    exterrors.Stage(exterrors.WithFlash(nil)),
)</code></pre>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-50">Safe messages and shared templates</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-slate-400">In the safe mode <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">Data.Message</code> is <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">exterrors.SafeMessage</code> and <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">Data.Location</code> is empty, so an error template can print the message; <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">WithMessage(fn)</code> sets it, such as to a translation in the request's language, for the error page, the fragment and the error flash message; <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">Data.Error</code> holds the error in both modes. A stage with only a page template answers partial requests with it too, and one with only a fragment template full pages. The error template renders as a child of the partial that failed, so a root with <code class="rounded border border-sky-400/20 bg-sky-400/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-sky-100">SetChildIncludes("partials/*.gohtml")</code> gives it the partials of the pages.</p>
</article>

